# Abnormal Uterine Bleeding - Literature Search on UpToDate <span class="tag" tag-name="Reference"><span class="smallcaps">Reference</span></span>

- **References:**
  - [UpToDate: Causes of female genital tract bleeding](https://www.uptodate.com.eproxy.lib.hku.hk/contents/causes-of-female-genital-tract-bleeding?sectionName=Uterine+bleeding&search=per+vaginal+bleeding&topicRef=3263&anchor=H2&source=see_link#H2)
  - [UpToDate: Abnormal uterine bleeding in nonpregnant reproductive-age patients: Terminology, evaluation, and approach to diagnosis](https://www.uptodate.com.eproxy.lib.hku.hk/contents/abnormal-uterine-bleeding-in-nonpregnant-reproductive-age-patients-terminology-evaluation-and-approach-to-diagnosis?search=per%20vaginal%20bleeding&source=search_result&selectedTitle=3~150&usage_type=default&display_rank=3)
  - [UpToDate: Abnormal uterine bleeding in nonpregnant reproductive-age patients: Management](https://www.uptodate.com.eproxy.lib.hku.hk/contents/abnormal-uterine-bleeding-in-nonpregnant-reproductive-age-patients-management?search=per%20vaginal%20bleeding&source=search_result&selectedTitle=2~150&usage_type=default&display_rank=2)
  - [UpToDate: Approach to the patient with postmenopausal uterine bleeding](https://www.uptodate.com.eproxy.lib.hku.hk/contents/approach-to-the-patient-with-postmenopausal-uterine-bleeding?search=per+vaginal+bleeding&topicRef=3263&source=see_link)

## Causes of female genital tract bleeding

- **Definition and terminology:**
  - <u>Per vaginal bleeding</u> (PV bleeding) - a presenting problem detected by the patient, but which may be of gynaecological origin (further classified into lower and upper genital tract causes), or non-gynaecological origin (PR bleeding, haematuria) that is mistakened by patients as PV bleeding
  - <u>Abnormal uterine bleeding</u> (AUB) - bleeding that is **assumed to arise from the uterus**
- **Etiology of PV bleeding:**
  - **Pregnancy-related** - early pregnancy complications (ectopic pregnancy, miscarriage), late pregnancy complications
  - **Non pregnancy-related:**
    - <u>Lower-genital tract bleeding</u> - cervical bleeding, vaginal bleeding, vulvar bleeding
    - <u>Upper-genital tract bleeding</u> - abnormal uterine bleeding, fallopian tube bleeding, ovarian bleeding
    - <u>Non-genital tract disease</u> (mistakened as PV bleeding) - PR bleeding, haematuria or skin conditions involving the perineum or skin beyond the vulva
- **Lower genital tract bleeding:**
  - **Cervical bleeding** - often presents w/ <u>sporadic spotting</u>, and <u>post-coital bleeding</u>, and the cause is easily identifiable on speculum examination:
    - <u>Benign lesions</u> - cervical polyps, cervical ectropion, pelvic organ prolase (ulcerations), cervicitis (strawberry red spots if Trichomonas vaginalis)
    - <u>Malignant lesions</u> - cervical cancer, direct extension from other gynaecological cancers (e.g. endometrial carcinoma)
  - **Vaginal bleeding** - presents similar as cervical bleeding w/ <u>sporadic spotting</u>, and <u>post-coital bleeding</u>, however trauma-associated vaginal bleeding can cause major haemorrhage:
    - <u>Trauma</u>:
      - Intercourse-related - e.g. coitarche (tearing of intact hymen), sexual assault
      - Non-intercourse-related - e.g. pelvic trauma, straddle-type injuries
    - <u>Infection/ inflammation</u> - vaginitis, vaginal ulcers
    - <u>Benign growths</u> - vaginal polyps, gartner duct cysts, vaginal adenosis
    - <u>Atrophic vaginitis</u> (i.e. genitourinary syndrome) - a/w hypoestrogenic changes
    - <u>Malignant growths</u> - primary vaginal cancer
  - **Vulvar bleeding** - often presents w/ <u>sporadic bleeding</u>, but can be directly visualised on infection:
    - <u>Benign lesions</u> - STI, candida vulvovaginitis, condylomata acuminatum etc.
    - <u>Malignant lesions</u> - primary vulvar cancer
- **Upper genital tract bleeding:**
  - **Uterine bleeding** - clinical presentation of AUB is extremely variable, but is a deviation of normal menstrual cycle (by frequency, regularity, duration, flow, intermenstrual bleeding or unscheduled bleeding), and is classified by FIGO PALM-COEIN system (see below)
  - **Fallopian tube bleeding** - rarely presenting as vaginal bleeding but are caused by **tubal pregnancy**, infections (PID), malignancies
  - **Ovarian bleeding** - rarely presenting as vaginal bleeding (more commonly intra-peritoneal bleeding), but theoretically possible via the fallopian tube
- **Normal menstrual parameters vs abnormal uterine bleeding** - by 1) frequency, 2) regularity, 3) duration, 4) flow, and 5) any unscheduled bleeding: 
![](~/Documents/notes/img/normal_menstrual_parameters.png)
- **Causes of abnormal uterine bleeding** (AUB) - FIGO PALM-COIN system stratifies causes into structural and functional: 
![](~/Documents/notes/img/figo_palm_coein_system.png)
  - **Structural causes** (PALM):
    - <u>Polyps</u> (AUB-P) - benign polyps of the endometrium w/ increasing prevalence w/ age, typically presenting as **intermenstrual bleeding**, but rarely HMB, post-coital bleeding, postmenopausal bleeding etc.
    - <u>Adenomyosis</u> (AUB-A) - presence of endometrial glands within uterine myometrium, typically presenting w/ 1) **HMB**, 2) **secondary dysmenorrhoea**, and 3) **enlarged uterus** on P/E
    - <u>Leiomyomas</u> (AUB-L) - fibroids usually present w/ **HMB**, as presence of large fibroid results in increased surface area for endometrial growth and heavier flow
    - <u>Malignancy and hyperplasia</u> (AUB-M) - endometrial hyperplasia or endometrial carcinoma (or other rare uterine cancers \[e.g. uterine sarcomas, GTN\]), typically presents as 1) **post-menopausal bleeding**, or 2) intermenstrual bleeding in pre-menopausal women
  - **Functional causes** (COEIN):
    - <u>Coagulopathy</u> (AUB-C) - disorders of primary or secondary haemostasis may present with **HMB**, and suspicion should be raised when <u>bleeding diathesis</u> is elicited from Hx taking (N.B. note correlation of bleeding severity w/ estrogen levels \[e.g. bleeding tendancies masked by COC pills\])
    - <u>Ovarian dysfunction</u> (AUB-O) - non-cyclical production of estrogen and progesterone due to dysfunction of HPO axis resulting in excessive endometrial growth and shedding, often presenting with irregular, prolonged or heavy menstrual bleeding
    - <u>Endometrial dysfunction</u> (AUB-E) - disorders of local endometrial haemostasis or prior inflammatory or infective endometrial disorders resulting in presentation as 1) **HMB**, and 2) **IMB**
    - <u>Iatrogenic causes</u> (AUB-I) - drug Tx such as contraception, anti-coagulation, replacement hormonal therapy, or drugs that cause hyperprolactinaemia
    - <u>Not otherwise-specified</u> (AUB-N) - unclassified entities inclduing Cesarean scar defect, AV malformation, endometrial myometrial vascularity or others
- **Ovarian dysfunction** - endocrine causes of anovulation and oligo-anovulation in a non-pregnant reproductive-age patient:
  - **Pathophysiology of AUB-O** - estrogen and progesterones are not produced cyclically:
    - <u>Unopposed estrogen production</u> - estrogen continues to be produced but are un-opposed by progesterone allowing for continued proliferation of the endometrium
    - <u>Absence of prostaglandin-related changes</u> - shedding is thus not uniform, and is often irregular, prolonged and heavy
  - **Clinical features of AUB-O:**
    - Irregular menses prior to amenorrhoea
    - Prolonged and heavy bleeding
  - **Etiology of AUB** - caused by dysruption:
    - **Hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism:**
      - <u>Structural hypothalamic/ pituitary disease</u> - e.g. neoplastic (adenoma, meningioma), infiltrative (e.g. haemosiderosis, Langher's cell histiocytosis histiocytosis, hypophysitis), traumatic (TBI/ parasellar surgery)
      - <u>Functional gonadotrophin deficiency</u>:
        - Anorexia nervosa
        - Excessive physical exercise
        - Psychological stress
        - Other endocrinopathies - e.g. **primary hypothyroidism**, Cushing's syndrome, Hyperprolactinaemia, hyper-androgenism
        - Chronic systemic illness (e.g. malabsorption, renal failure)
      - <u>Isolated gonadotrophic deficiency</u> - e.g. Kallmann's syndrome
    - **Ovarian dysfunction:**
      - <u>Hypergonadotrophic hypogonadism</u> - acquired or congenital gonadal disorders
      - <u>Polycystic ovary syndrome</u> - characterised by sonographic features of the polycystic ovaries and biochemical/clinical hyperandrogenism

## Abnormal uterine bleeding in nonpregnant reproductive-age patients: Terminology, evaluation, and approach to diagnosis

- **Abnormal uterine bleeding** - reflects abnormalities in frequency, regularity, duration, flow, or intermenstrual bleeding:
  - <u>Frequent menstrual bleeding</u> - refers to periods that start at intervals of \< 24d
  - <u>Irregular bleeding</u> - increased cycle length variance, which is dependent on patient age:
    - 18-25y - cycle length variance \> 9d
    - 26-41y - cycle length variance \> 7d
    - 42-45y - cycle length variance \> 9d
  - <u>Prolonged menstrual bleeding</u> - refers to menstrual bleeding consistently lasting for \> 8d (but not always a/w HMB)
  - <u>Heavy menstrual bleeding</u> - excessive total volume of menstrual blood flow irrespective of duration, frequency or regularity, defined objectively as \> 80 ml/L, but clinically defined as any volume that interferes w/ patient's physical, social, and psychological QoL
  - <u>Intermenstrual bleeding</u> - AUB that occurs in between well-defined cyclical menses, which may be cyclical or acyclical:
    - Cyclical midcycle IMB - refers to small amounts of bleeding mid cycle attributed to midcycle drop of circulating E2 levels just after ovulation
    - Acyclical intermenstrual bleeding - bleeding in between cyclincal menses that are less predictable (often aw non-malignant lesions)
- **Approach to AUB in non-pregnant reproductive-age patients** - dependent on haemodynamic stability:
  - <u>Haemodynamically unstable patients</u> - requires ersuscitation and urgent admisison prior to workup
  - <u>Haemodynamically stable patients</u> - evaluation by detailed Hx, P/E and Ix
- **Salients points of Hx:**
  - **Recent or current pregnancy** - bleeding is common in all trimesters of pregnancy and after pregnancy loss or termination
  - **HPI and menstrual Hx:**
    - <u>Ensure PV bleeding</u> - is the patient certain bleeding is from the vagina:
      - Blood in toilet after urination (suggestive of haematuria)
      - Blood in toilet after defecation (suggestive of PR bleeding)
      - Blood when wiping (suggestive of anything around the perineum)
      - Blood on tampon/ pads (note the site)
    - <u>Onset and duration</u> - how long the patient has noticed the problem
    - <u>Quality</u> - assess the **pattern of bleeding**:
      - **Frequent menstrual bleeding** - patient suspects an increase in frequency of menstruation
      - **Heavy menstrual bleeding** - suspects an increase in frequency in flow
      - **Intermenstrual bleeding** - bleeding in between menstrual periods
    - <u>Timing of bleeding</u>:
      - **Post-coital bleeding** - consider vaginal or cervical sources of bleeding
      - **instrumentation** - new onset bbleeding in relation w/ instrumentation (e.g. TOP, IUD) localises to the uterus
  - **Menstrual Hx:**
    - <u>LMP</u> - note first day of last menstrual periods and several previous menstrual periods (for assessment of frequency and regularity)
    - <u>Menarche and menopause</u> - estimate estrogen exposure
    - <u>Cycle length and regularity</u> - characterize previous and current cycle length and regularity
    - <u>Menstrual flow</u> - characterize previous and current flow objectively by VAS or by frequency of changing pads/ tampons (consider whether full-soaked when changing)
    - <u>Anaemic Sx</u> - physical impact of AUB
  - **Sexual Hx** - consider pregnancy, or STI/ PID
  - **Past gynaecological Hx:**
    - <u>Cervical smear Hx</u> - past excisional cervical procedures or Mx of CIN suggests possibility of recurrent or persistent cervical neoplasia
    - <u>Myomectomy</u> - past Hx of myomectomy suggestive of recurrent or persistent uterine fibroids
  - **Past obstetrics Hx** - consider cesarian scar defect, or uterine surgery
  - **Associated Sx:**
    - <u>Abdominal pain</u> - ongoing abdominal/ pelvic pain should raise suspicion of pregnancy complications, or pelvic inflammatory disease
    - <u>Vaginal discharge</u> - presence of vaginal discharge localises to an infective or non-infective gynaecological cause
    - <u>Dysmenorrhoea</u> - pain that precedes periods are classical of endometriosis, while new-onset pain (secondary dysmenorrhoea) may be associated w/ adenomyosis
    - <u>Other bleeding manifestations</u> - e.g. recent bruising, epistaxis, PR bleeding
    - <u>Other Sx of endocrinopathies</u> (if appropriate) - Sx of hyper/ hypothyroidism, Sx of PCOS
  - **PMH:**
    - <u>Enodocrine disorders</u> - e.g. thyroid disorders, Cushing's syndrome, hyperprolactinaemia
    - <u>Bleeding disorders</u> - e.g. known bleeding disorders, FHx of bleeding disorders
  - **Drug Hx:**
    - <u>Drugs affecting haemostasis</u> - e.g. anti-platelets and anticoagulants
    - <u>Estrogen</u> - e.g. COC pills
    - <u>Drugs causing hyperprolactinaemia</u> - e.g. typical anti-psychotics, antidepressants
- **P/E:**
  - **General examination** - for bleeding tendancies, goitre, or evidence of hyperandrogenism (e.g. hirsutism, acne, clitoromegaly)
  - **Pelvic examination:**
    - <u>Inspection</u> - potential sites of bleeding in lower genital tract and perineum (+/- active bleeding)
    - <u>Speculum examination</u> - for any vision lesions in the vaginal wall and cervix
    - <u>Bimanual examination</u>:
      - **Size** - enlargement of uterus a/w pregnancy, adenomyosis (diffuse), fibroids (irregular), or ocassionally malignancy
      - **Mobility** - fixed uterus suggestive of pelvic adhesions (e.g. PID, endometriosis)
      - **Adnexal mass** - e.g. tubo-ovarian abscess
- **Initial Ix:**
  - <u>Urine pregnancy test</u> - r/o pregnancy; if +ve work up along the lines of pregnancy-related per-vaginal bleeding
  - <u>Pelvic USG</u> - typically as initial for identification of structural causes of AUB
  - <u>Endometrial sampling</u> - if presenting w/ intermenstrual bleeding, post-menopausal bleeding, and patients w/ HMB w/ many risk factors (to detect endometrial carcinoma)
- **Pelvic USG** - typically if uterus not palpable:
  - <u>Indications</u> - typically indicated for most patients presenting w/ HMB, IMB
  - <u>Findings</u> - abnormalities may be focal or diffuse:
    - Focal intracavitary pathology (e.g. endometrial polyps, or fibroids)
    - Diffuse endometrial thickening (definition of thickened endometrium depends on menopausal status)
  - <u>Follow-up studies</u>:
    - Saline-infused sonography
    - Diagnostic hysteroscopy
- **Endometrial sampling:**
  - <u>Indications</u> - for all thought to be at risk of endometrial hyperplasia/ neoplasia:
    - Presentation w/ intermenstrual bleeding
    - Higher age (e.g. \> 45y)
    - Persistent HMB w/ risk factors of endometrial cancer (e.g. obesity, chronic ovulatory dysfunction, tamoxifen)
    - Abnormal findings on pelvic USG
  - <u>Procedure</u> - blind sampling by pipelle
- **Additional Ix** - dependent on <u>pattern of bleeding</u>:
  - <u>Heavy menstrual bleeding or intermenstrual bleeding</u> - CBC, clotting profile, SIS, endometrial sampling
  - <u>Irregular bleeding, oligomenorrhoea or amenorrhoea</u> - hormone profile (FSH, LH, E2, testosterone, PRL, TFT)
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>Dx and Mx of underlying cause</u> - e.g. r/o all structural or functional causes of AUB
    - <u>General medical therapy</u> - e.g. iron replacement if anaemic, hormonal therapy to regulate menstrual cycle

## Abnormal uterine bleeding in nonpregnant reproductive-age patients: Management

- **Principles of Mx:**
  - **Goals of Mx:**
    - <u>Dx and Mx of underlying cause</u> - e.g. identification and Mx of endometrial hyperplasia/ carcinoma; identification of underlying bleeding diathesis; underlying endocrine pathology
    - <u>General medical Mx</u> - establish regular bleeding pattern or amenorrhoea by medical or hormonal therapy; Tx underlying iron-deficiency anaemia
  - **Factors influencing Mx:**
    - <u>Severity of bleeding</u> - acute uterine bleeding may require urgent evaluation and haemostasis
    - <u>Indications for Tx</u> - Tx of AUB should be individualised and is not necessary unless AUB interfering w/ daily activities
    - <u>Medical comorbidities</u> - hormonal therapy may be C/I in estrogen-containing Tx
    - <u>Plans for pregnancy</u> - as hormonal therapies may provide contraception as well
  - **Medical therapies for no known causes of AUB:**
    - <u>Hormonal therapies</u> - e.g. COC pills, Merina, oral progestin, non-contraceptive estrogen-progestin formulations
    - <u>Non-hormonal therapy</u> - tranexamic acid, NSAIDs

    
    ![](~/Documents/notes/img/selection_of_therapies_for_aub.png)
  - **Surgical Mx** - endometrial ablation, hysterectomy
- **COC pills:**
  - <u>Indications</u> - indicated for most patients w/ absence of infective, endocrine or structural etiologies w/ **no contraindications to estrogen:**
    - HMB - reduces menstrual flow
    - Anovulation (AUB-O) - cycle regulation and prevention of endometrial hyperplasia and carcinoma
  - <u>Schedule</u> - COC pills can be prescribed in:
    - Cyclic dosing (withdrawal bleeding every 1 mo)
    - Extended dosing (withdrawal bleeding every 3 mo)
    - Continuous dosing (no withdrawal bleeding)
  - <u>C/I for estrogen</u>:
    - **Increased risk of venous thromboembolism:**
      - Past Hx or FHx of VTE
      - Known inherited thrombophilia
      - Other thrombophilic states (e.g. active malignancy, nephrotic syndrome, anti-phospholipid syndrome)
    - **Increased risk of arterial thromboembolism:**
      - <u>Known cardiovascular or cerebrovascular disease</u> - known IHD, Hx of stroke or TIA, complicated valvular heart disease (e.g. resulting in AF)
      - <u>Known risk factors of atherosclerosis</u> - smoking (heavy smoker \> 35y), DM, HTN (poorly controlled), obesity
      - <u>Migraine w/ aura</u> - C/I at any age as increased risk of stroke
- **Levonorgestrel IUD** (Merina):
  - <u>Indications</u> - typically first-line Tx for those where COC pills are contraindicated and do not desire to conceive
  - <u>C/I for IUD</u>:
    - Plans for pregnancy
    - Active pelvic infections
    - Anatomically distorted uterine cavity (since high risk of expulsion)
- **Tranexamic acid:**
  - <u>MOA</u> - anti-fibrinolytic:
    - Prevents conversion of plasminogen into plasmin
    - Reduces plasmin effect on fibrinolysis
  - <u>Dosing and duration</u> - 1300 mg tid for as long as 5d during menstruation (dose-adjustment for those w/ impaired kidney function)
  - <u>C/I</u>:
    - History of thrombosis
    - Known inherited or acquired thrombophilia (e.g. active malignancy, APLS)
  - <u>S/E</u> - generally well tolerated:
    - N/V
    - Headaches
    - Dysmeonorrhoea (as TXA has no effect on reducing dysmenorrhoea)
- **NSAIDs:**
  - <u>MOA</u> - reduced PGE2 synthesis in endometrium, resulting in vasoconstriction
  - <u>Dosing and regimen</u> - taken on first day of bleeding and continued for 2-3 d:
    - Ibupprofen 600 mg bid-qid
    - Naproxen 500 mg at onset and 3-5h later, then 250-500 mg bid
    - Mefenamic acid 500 mg bid
  - <u>C/I</u> - concern for bleeding disorders

## Approach to the patient with postmenopausal uterine bleeding

- **Definition** - any uterine uterine bleeding in a menopausal patient, except for those w/ withdrawal bleeding due to cyclic post-menopausal hormonal therapy
- **Epidemiology:**
  - <u>Prevalence</u> - occurs in 4-11% of post-menopausal bleeding
- **Etiology of PMB** - list of DDx is less broad than that in pre-menopausal women:
  - **Benign lesions:**
    - <u>Endometrial polyps</u> - localised hyperplastic overgrowths of endometrial glands
    - <u>Atrophic vaginitis</u> - hypoestrogenic changes in the endometrium and vagina where intracavitary friction results in micro-erosions resulting in minor bleeding or spotting
    - <u>Fibroids</u> - typically resolve during menopause as leiomyomas are estrogen-sensitive tumours, but may be symptomatic in those on HRT
    - <u>Adenomyosis</u> - esp. if Hx of dysmenorrhoea and HMB in pre-menopausal state, but are only symptomatic during post-menopausal phase in those on HRT
  - **Pre-malignant and Malignant lesions:**
    - <u>Endometrial hyperplasia</u> - w/ or w/o atypia, seen in perimenopausal and post-menopausal women
    - <u>Endometrial carcinoma</u> - esp. in post-menopausal women w/ adequate risk factors (e.g. unopposed estrogen exposure)
    - <u>Cervical cancer</u> - typically present w/ post-coital bleeding
    - <u>Upper genital tract carcinoma</u> - rare mode of presentation in epithelial carcinomas of the ovary, fallopian tube and peritoneum
- **Salient points of Hx:**
  - **HPI** - onset, progression, quality, severity, timing:
    - <u>Onset</u> - acute vs chronic
    - <u>Progression</u> - static vs progressive
    - <u>Quality and severity</u> - spotting vs bleeding
    - <u>Timing</u> - a/w trauma, intercourse
  - **Associated Sx:**
    - <u>Fever</u> - suggestive of infective etiology
    - <u>Pain</u> - note site and timing of pain (e.g. vaginal dryness, soreness w/ dyspareunia a/w atrophy)
    - <u>Vaginal discharge</u> - seen in various gynaecological conditions
    - <u>Urinary Sx</u> - points towards a pelvic mass
    - <u>Bowel Sx</u> - points towards a pelvic mass
  - **Menstrual Hx:**
    - <u>Menarche and menopause</u> - estimate of duration of estrogen exposure (although not unopposed)
    - <u>Cycle length and regularity</u> - irregularity suggestive of anovulatory cycle (e.g. by PCOS) which results in **unopposed estrogen cycle**
    - <u>Previous menstrual flow</u> - any HMB, or AUB (e.g. PCB, IMB) in pre-menopausal state
    - <u>Dysmenorrhoea</u> - secondary dysmenorrhoea a/w adenomyosis
  - **Cervical smear Hx** - whether performed or any atypical findings
  - **PMH:**
    - <u>Breast cancer</u> - adjuvant hormonal therapy w/ tamoxifen is a risk factor for endometrial carcinoma
    - <u>Gynaecological conditions</u> - Hx of ovulatory dysfunction a/w increased risk for endometrial carcinoma
  - **Drug Hx** - antiplatelets, anticoagulants, HRT
  - **SHx** - smoking, alcohol
  - **FHx** - breast, endometrial and colorectal cancer (Lynch syndrome)
- **P/E:**
  - <u>General examination</u> - bleeding tendancy, BMI
  - <u>Pelvic examination</u>:
    - Speculum examination for any abnormal cervical lesion (Bx if visible), and pap-smear
    - Bimanual examination for uterus size, mobility, and adnexal masses
- **Ix:**
  - **Cervical pap smear** - for evaluation of cervical cancer
  - **Transvaginal USG** - usually initial Ix if suspected post-menopausal uterine bleeding
  - **Endometrial sampling** - indicated even if TVUSS is inconclusive
  - **Endometrial Bx** - enables histological Dx

# Approach to Pelvic Mass and Conditions of the Pelvis and Ovaries - Literature Search on UpToDate <span class="tag" tag-name="Reference"><span class="smallcaps">Reference</span></span>

# GC118 Pelvic Mass, Ovarian Cancer, Cysts, Uterine Fibroids <span class="tag" tag-name="Lecture"><span class="smallcaps">Lecture</span></span>

- **Dysmenorrhoea:**
  - Primary - early onset, need to consider structural abnormality
  - Secondary - no previous Hx of dysmenorrhoea, and consider other Sx (e.g. menorrhagia, subfertility), consider adenomyosis if pelvic mass present

## Approach to pelvic mass

- **DDx of pelvic mass** - Gynaecological vs non-gynaecological:
  - **Gynaecological:**
    - <u>Pregnancy</u> - undiagnosed pregnancy, molar pregnancy
    - <u>Ovaries</u> - ovarian cyst, paraovarian cyst, hydrosalpinx
    - <u>Uterus</u> - fibroid, adenomyosis
    - Malignancies - ovarian cancer, uterine sarcoma, metastatic (Krukenberg)
  - **Non-gynaecological:**
    - <u>GI</u> - mesenteric cysts, tumor, hernia, diverticulum, dilated bowel
    - <u>Urological</u> - bladder, diverticulum, pelvic kidney and transplanted kidney
    - <u>Retroperitoneal</u> - sarcoma (usually not palpable)
    - Others - e.g. pseudocyst, abcess
- **Salient points of Hx:**
  - **Demographic** - age implicates different DDx
  - **HPI** - site, onset, progression, how the mass was discovered:
    - <u>Site</u> - central or lateralised
    - <u>Onset and duration</u> - duration of the mass noticed (+ how was the mass noticed)
    - <u>Progression</u> - change in size over time
  - **Associated Sx:**
    - <u>Abdominal pain</u> - differentiate chronic pain vs sudden acute pain which may reflect surgical emergency
    - <u>Vaginal bleeding</u> - PV bleeding raises suspicion of early complications of pregnancy if a/w pain, or other gynaecological malignancies
    - <u>Abdominal distension</u> - common in different gynaecological and non-gynaecological disease
    - <u>Urinary Sx</u> (e.g. frequency, urgency) - due to pressure effect or invasion but can arise from both gynaecological and non-gynaecological etiology
    - <u>Bowel Sx</u> (e.g. altered bowel habit) - due to pressure effect or invasion of gynaecological disease, but may reflect a primary GI pathology
    - <u>Constitutional Sx</u> (e.g. weight loss, unexplained fever) - suggestive of underlying malignancy (gynaecological or non-gynaecological)
  - **Menstrual Hx** - more related to benign conditions of the uterus, and an estimation of estrogen exposure:
    - <u>LMP</u> - note that recent date of LMP may in fact be PV bleeding and thus cannot r/o early complications of pregnancy (confirm w/ the period before LMP)
    - <u>Cycle length and regularity</u> - increased cycle length and irregularity raises suspicion of uterine pathologies
    - <u>Flow and duration</u> - HMB raises suspicion of fibroids
    - <u>Dysmenorrhoea</u> - raises suspicion of adenomyosis if a/w secondary dysmenorrhoea
    - <u>Anaemic Sx</u> - caused by iron-deficiency anaemia caused by AUB
  - **Other Hx as relevant** - previous gynae Hx, cervical smear, obstetrics Hx, sexual and contraceptive Hx, SHx, FHx
- **P/E** - general examination, abdominal examination, pelvic examination, PR examination:
  - <u>General examination</u> - Vital signs, pregnancy test, BMI, performance status, Pallor, LN, LL oedema
  - <u>Abdominal examination</u>:
    - Inspection for mass, distension and nodules (Sistery Mary Joseph nodule)
    - Mass and localised tenderness
    - Shifting dullness
  - <u>Pelvic examination</u> - bimanual examination to detect:
    - Uterus size, orientation and mobility
    - Adnexal mass
    - Pouch of Douglas for nodularity, thickening, and tenderness
  - <u>PR examination</u> - for pouch of Douglas, any palpable mass in Blummer's shelf
  - <u>Systems review</u> - chest examination
- **Clinical approach to pelvic mass:**
  - Identify unstable patients - S/S shock and peritonism requires straight laparotomy
  - Exclude ovarian cyst complications and preganancy complications
- **Ix:**
  - **Pelvic USG** - most important for visualisation of pelvic mass
  - **Urine pregnancy test** - to r/o early pregnancy complications
  - **Routine bloods** - CBC, RFT:
    - <u>CBC</u> - anaemia if a/w menorrhagia
    - <u>RFT</u> - if mass causes hydronephrosis
  - **Additional Ix** - depending on underlying cause:
    - Ca 125 for suspected malignancy
    - Staging imaging for suspected ovarian cancer (e.g. CT/ MRI/ PET-CT)
    - Pre-opereative workup individualised if presenting as a surgical case

## Approach to adnexal mass

- **DDx of adnexal mass** - benign vs malignant:
  - **Benign:**
    - <u>Functional</u> - follicular cyst, corpus luteal cysttheca luteal cyst
    - <u>Inflammatory</u> - endometrioma, tubo-ovarian abscess
    - <u>Germ cell</u> - mature teratoma
    - <u>Epithelial</u> - serous cystadenoma, mucinous cystadenoma, clear cell cystadenoma
    - <u>Sex cord stromal</u> - fibroma, theocoma
    - <u>Others</u> - **ovarian ectopic pregnancy**
  - **Malignant** - primary vs metastatic:
    - <u>Epithelial tumours</u> (arising in ovaries, fallopian tube) - serous, mucinous, endometrioid, clear cells
    - <u>Germ cell tumours</u> - e.g. dysgerminoma, yolk sac tumour, immature teratoma
    - <u>Sex-cord stroma tumour</u> - granulosa cell tumour, sertoli-leydig cell

    
    ![](~/Documents/notes/img/types_of_malignant_ovarian_tumours.png)
- **Pelvic USG** - sonographic evaluation of the adnexal mass:
  - <u>Sonographic features of an adnexal mass</u>:
    - Size
    - Laterality (bilateral vs unilateral)
    - Cyst content
    - Septations
    - Presence of ascites
    - Doppler flow
  - <u>Echogenicity</u>:
    - Anechoic
    - Homogenous hypoechogenicity
    - Homogenous hyperechogenicity
    - Hterogenous echogenicity - may be haemorrhagic, mixed fluid (blood or fat), abscess, or as seen in teratoma
  - <u>Cyst contents</u> - may be solid or cystic
  - <u>International ovarian tumor analysis ultrasound rules to classify mass as benign or malignant</u> - 95% Sn, 91% Sp: 
  ![](~/Documents/notes/img/b_rule_and_m_rule_for_ovarian_tumour.png)
    - **Classify malignant if** - \>= 1 M feature in absence of B feature
    - **Classify benign if** - \>= 1 B feature in absence of M feature
    - **Inconclusive if** - no B or M features present, or if both B or M features are present
  - <u>Risk of malignancy index</u> - based on sonographic featueres, post-menopausal state, and CA 125: 
  ![](~/Documents/notes/img/rmi_for_ovarian_tumour.png)
- **CA-125** (tumour marker) - celomic epithelium related glycoprotein that is present in most serous, endometrioid and clear cell ovarian carcinomas (less common in mucinous tumours):
  - <u>Cutoff</u> - 35 u/ml
  - <u>Test properties</u>:
    - Low Sp
    - More Sn and Sp in post-menopausal women
  - <u>Limitations</u> - elevated in:
    - Menstruating and pregnant women
    - Benign ovarian tumours (10%)
    - Endometriosis (20-30%)
    - Fibroids
    - Acute PID
    - Liver cirrhosis (60-70%)
    - Pancreatitis (30%)
  - <u>Role</u> - non diagnstic; mainly utilised for assessment of Tx response or recurrence
- **ROMA** (Risk of Ovarian Malignancy) - predictive scoring system by differentiating low-risk and high-risk patients, and incorporates 1) menopausal state, 2) CA-125, 3) HE4 (adjunct tumour marker):
  - <u>Score calculation</u>: 
  ![](~/Documents/notes/img/roma_for_ovarian_malignancy.png)
  - <u>Test-properties</u>: 
  ![](~/Documents/notes/img/roma_performance_in_differentiating_patients_with_ovarian_malignancy.png)
- **ADNEX model** - predictive model for ovarian cancer based on sonographic features, and serum CA-125 (N.B. BMJ systematic review Barrenada 2024)
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx** - dependent on 1) menopausal state, 2) symptoms, 3) risk of malignancy:
    - **Pre-menopausal:**
      - <u>Asymptomatic and unlikely malignancy</u> - observe and repeat USG 3-6 mo (consider surgical removal and Dx if persistent)
      - <u>Symptomatic</u> - consider surgiacl removal
      - <u>High risk of malignancy</u> - refer to oncology and exclude secondary from colon, stomach, and breast
    - **Post-menopausal** - conservative only if very low risk (e.g. asymptomatic, RMI I \< 200, unilocular cyst \< 5cm, unchanged after serial evaluation): 
    ![](~/Documents/notes/img/approach_to_postmenopausal_ovarian_cyst.png)

## Fibroids

- **Clinical presentation of fibroids:**
  - asymptomatic
  - Menorrhagia - if subserosal and invades endometrial cavity (increased surface area of endometrium)
  - Abdominal distension (Meig syndrome)
  - Pressure effect:
    - Urinary Sx - if anterior and lower, compression on the bladder, resultsin in freuquency, urgency, and urge incontinence
    - Bowel Sx - if posterior of uterus, compression onto rectum, results in tenesmus sensation
  - Anaemia Sx
  - Dysmenorrhoea can happen but not typical (consider adenomyosis as other DDx) - mechanistically due to similar mechanism in clot colic
- **Signs of fibroid:**
  - General examination - pallor
  - Abdominal examination - soft, non-tender
  - Pelvis - firm mass arising from pelvis, rubebery consistency (e.g. symmetric/ assymetric/ irregular) and moves with cervix
- **Classification** - based on histology (see slides)
- **Ix:**
  - Pelvic USG (+/- sonohysterogram for fibroid polyp) - well circumscribed lesions with pseudocapsule that has heterogenous echogenicity
- **Pathological changes of fibroids** - read slides and look for clinical significance

## Ovarian cyst

- **Clinical features of ovarian cyst:**
  - Asymptomatic
  - Meig syndrome
  - Pressure Sx
  - Abdominal pain - due to mass effect or complications:
    - Sudden pain if related to complications - e.g. torsion, haemorrhage, rupture
    - Chronic pelvic pain
  - +/- dysmenorrhoea - if endometrioma
- **Signs:**
  - Vitals - check vitals if acute pain
  - Abdomen - peritoneal signs, usually no ascites
  - Pelvic examination:
    - Mobility - dermoid cyst mobile, endometrioma immobile due to adhesions
    - Tenderness
    - Consistency
  - +/- Chest examination - Meig syndrome
- Types of ovarian masses (table in slides)

## Ovarian cancer

- **Clinical features of ovarian cancer:**
  - Asymptomatic
  - Non-specific GI upset
  - Abdominal distension
  - Abdominal pain
  - Pressure symptoms
  - Complications - rupture/ haemorrhage
  - Systemic Sx - e.g. pyrexia of unknown origin, anorexia and weight loss, LL swelling, LN
  - Sx related to metastasis
- **Signs** - most present late:
  - General examination - cachexic
  - Signs of metastasis:
    - LN - SCLN
    - DVT
    - Pleural effusion (?Meig syndrome)
    - Abdominal organomegaly
    - Mobile/ fixed non-tender mass arising from pelvis
    - Deposits in PoD
